## Automated Demo Mode — how it runs

A single toggle within Developer Mode, off by default and requiring explicit confirmation to enable (e.g. a confirm dialog: *"Demo Mode will run automatically and may place real calls and send real SMS when it triggers an emergency. Continue?"*).

When enabled:

- A timer (configurable interval, e.g. every 20–40s) selects a normal reading and pushes it into `VitalsProvider`, keeping the dashboard/ring updating continuously.
- Separately, an abnormal reading is injected roughly **every 1–2 minutes** (randomized within that window so it's not predictable to the second), picked from the abnormal scenarios in the dataset (resting emergency, exercise-context spike, or ambiguous/override).
- Normal readings simply update the dashboard/ring, no alert.
- Abnormal readings run through the **real, unmodified** escalation path — actual phone call, actual SMS, actual location — not a simulated/blocked trigger. This is the point: prove the full pipeline fires for real without a human in the loop.
- **Hard cooldown is mandatory.** Once an emergency has been triggered and is unresolved (or was just resolved), Demo Mode must not fire another abnormal reading until a configurable cooldown period has passed (e.g. 2 minutes), even if the abnormal-injection timer would otherwise select one. This prevents repeated real calls/SMS from firing back-to-back.
- While an alert is active (countdown running or unresolved), Demo Mode pauses its own scheduling entirely — it does not queue or stack triggers.
- The "DEMO MODE ACTIVE" banner must be visible anywhere simulated data is driving the UI (dashboard, vitals ring, alert screens) — non-negotiable, so simulated data is never mistakable for real monitoring.
- Turning Demo Mode off mid-countdown cancels any active timer/alert cleanly — no orphaned state.

